855067985307e-07,-0.922130823135376,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-0.020587211474776268,-0.13201197981834412,0.0,0.0,-0.00029202012228779495,0.0,-0.0005463061388581991,0.0,0.0,-7.295342220459133e-05,0.0,-9.536738616588991e-07,0.0,0.0,-2.622600959512056e-06,-0.002612750744447112,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.0006291079334914684,-4.768370445162873e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-9.536738616588991e-07,-1.0728830375228426e-06,-8.344646857949556e-07,-2.622600959512056e-06,-3.576278118089249e-07,-2.50339189733495e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,1555,5733,1506,553,198,59,26056,10374,13233,90,85,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,488,259,24984,220,18,83,24984,220,16,481,259,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,60,48101,304,264,11031,315,279,1352,400,87,488,379,488,17988,284,294,2418,220,11252,279,11457,6716,4930,66,11991,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe line parameterized by\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\]lies in a plane of the form $x + y + cz = d.$  Enter the ordered pair $(c,d).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"85b1c0e331e0ed798c0aaa2afa1023706b9842ceaf3f9ed294ff88558bb5b7c1"},"seconds":22.92615842819214,"seed":451267659395531466,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nHowever, we already have the direction vector of the line, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Since the line lies in the plane, the direction vector of the line is parallel to the plane and does not provide a new direction for the normal vector. We need a vector that is not parallel to the direction vector of the line. We can use the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (from the point \\((1,0,1)\\) to \\((1,0,0)\\)).\n\nThe normal vector \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) to the plane is the cross product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(3 \\cdot 0 - (-1) \\cdot 0) - \\mathbf{j}(1 \\cdot 0 - (-1) \\cdot 1) + \\mathbf{k}(1 \\cdot 0 - 3 \\cdot 1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the normal vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), so \\(c = -3\\).\n\nThe plane equation is \\(0x - y - 3z = d\\). Since the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane, we substitute \\(x = 1\\), \\(y = 0\\), and \\(z = 1\\) into the plane equation:\n\\[\n0(1) - 0 - 3(1) = d \\implies d = -3.\n\\]\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(-3, -3)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(-3, -3)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:264"}
